/*!
@defgroup linflexd_uart LINFlexD UART Driver (Linflexd_uart)
@ingroup linflexd_driver
@brief This section describes the programming interface of the Peripheral driver for LINFlexD in UART mode.

# UART Driver Overview {#UARTDRIVEROvw}

The LINFlexD in UART mode is used for serial communication. These sections
describe the YTMicro SDK software modules API that can be used for initializing and configuring the module, as well
as initiating serial communications using the interrupt-based method.<br />

# UART Driver Device structures {#UARTDRIVERDevS}

The driver uses instantiations of the linflexd_uart_state_t to maintain the current state of a particular UART Hardware instance
module driver.<br />
The user is required to provide memory for the driver state structures during the initialization. The driver itself
does not statically allocate memory.

# Integration guideline #

## Compilation units ##

The following files need to be compiled in the project:
\verbatim
${SDK_PATH}\platform\drivers\src\linflexd\linflexd_uart_driver.c
${SDK_PATH}\platform\drivers\src\linflexd\linflexd_uart_irq.c
\endverbatim

## Include path ##

The following paths need to be added to the include path of the toolchain:
\verbatim
${SDK_PATH}\platform\drivers\inc\
\endverbatim

## Compile symbols ##

No special symbols are required for this component

## Dependencies ##

- \ref clock_manager @n
- \ref interrupt_manager @n
- \ref osif @n

# UART Driver Initialization {#UARTDRIVERInit}

1. To initialize the UART driver, call the LINFlexD_UART_DRV_Init() function and pass the instance number of the relevant LINFlexD
hardware interface instance which is LINFlexD instance in this release.<br />
For example: to use LINFlexD0 pass value 0 to the initialization function.
2. Pass a user configuration structure #linflexd_uart_user_config_t as shown here:
~~~~~{.c}
/* UART Driver configuration structure */
typedef struct
{
    uint32_t baudRate;                             /*!< baud rate */
    bool parityCheck;                              /*!< parity control - enabled/disabled
                                                        NOTE: this parameter is valid only for 8/16 bits words;
                                                              for 7/15 word length parity is always enabled */
    linflexd_uart_parity_type_t parityType;        /*!< always 0/always 1/even/odd */
    linflexd_uart_stop_bits_count_t stopBitsCount; /*!< number of stop bits, 1 stop bit (default) or 2 stop bits */
    linflexd_uart_word_length_t wordLength;        /*!< number of bits per transmitted/received word */
    linflexd_uart_transfer_type_t txTransferType;    /*!< Type of UART tx transfer (interrupt/dma based) */
    linflexd_uart_transfer_type_t rxTransferType;    /*!< Type of UART rx transfer (interrupt/dma based) */
    uint8_t rxDMAChannel;                          /*!< Channel number for DMA rx channel.
                                                        If DMA mode is not used this field will be ignored. */
    uint8_t txDMAChannel;                          /*!< Channel number for DMA tx channel.
                                                        If DMA mode is not used this field will be ignored. */
} linflexd_uart_user_config_t;
~~~~~
3. For UART, typically the user configures the #linflexd_user_config_t instantiation with a baudrate.<br />
-E.g. 115200 bps uartInitConfig.baudRate = 115200U.
4. UART transfers with parity check or not.
5. Only parity check enbaled, parity type will take effect.<br />
-E.g. uartInitConfig.parityType = LINFlexD_UART_PARITY_EVEN
6. User can config stopBitsCount to change UART stop bits.<br />
-E.g. uartInitConfig.stopBitsCount = LINFlexD_UART_ONE_STOP_BIT.
7. User can config wordLength to change UART data length.<br />
-E.g. uartInitConfig.wordLength = LINFlexD_UART_8_BITS
8. UART tx transfer will base on txTransferType.
9. UART rx transfer will base on rxTransferType.
10. Only DMA rx transfer selected, rxDMAChannel will take effect and UART rx transfer will use the rxDMAChannel.
11. Only DMA tx transfer selected, txDMAChannel will take effect and UART tx transfer will use the txDMAChannel.
12. This is a code example to set up a user LINFlexD UART Driver configuration instantiation:
~~~~~{.c}
    /* Device instance number as UART instance*/
    #define LI0 (0U)

    linflexd_uart_state_t uartState;
    linflexd_uart_user_config_t uartUserConfig;
    /* Set baudrate 115200 bps */
    uartUserConfig.baudRate = 115200U;
    /* Parity check disable */
    uartUserConfig.parityCheck = false;
    /* Set stop bits 1-bit */
    uartUserConfig.stopBitsCount = LINFlexD_UART_ONE_STOP_BIT;
    /* Set word length 8-bits */
    uartUserConfig.wordLength = LINFlexD_UART_8_BITS;
    /* Set tx transfer based on interrupt */
    uartUserConfig.txTransferType = LINFlexD_UART_USING_INTERRUPTS;
    /* Set rx transfer based on interrupt */
    uartUserConfig.rxTransferType = LINFlexD_UART_USING_INTERRUPTS;

    /* Initialize LINFlexD Hardware interface */
    LINFlexD_UART_DRV_Init(LI0, (linflexd_uart_user_config_t *) &uartUserConfig, (linflexd_uart_state_t *) &uartState);
~~~~~

# UART Data Transfers {#UARTDRIVERTrnsf}

The driver implements transmit and receive functions to transfer buffers of data by blocking, polling and non-blocking modes.

The blocking transmit and receive functions include LINFlexD_UART_DRV_SendDataBlocking() and the LINFlexD_UART_DRV_ReceiveDataBlocking functions.

The polling transmit and receive functions include LINFlexD_UART_DRV_SendDataPolling() and the LINFlexD_UART_DRV_ReceiveDataPolling functions.

The non-blocking (async) transmit and receive functions include the LINFlexD_UART_DRV_SendData() and the LINFlexD_UART_DRV_ReceiveData functions.

The LINFlexD_UART_DRV_ReceiveData() function should be called before data is transferring on the LINFlexD bus.

## Note

1. When the vector table is not in ram (__flash_vector_table__ = 1):
    - INT_SYS_InstallHandler shall check if the function pointer provided as parameter for the new handler is already
    present in the vector table for the given IRQ number.
    - The user will be required to manually add the correct handlers in the startup files

*/